  | 218:### PWM: playing recordings on a speaker with two positions
  | 229:## Cheat sheet: C to QB64-PE
  | 242:## Library reference (`PCSPKR.BM`)
  | 252:* **Duke/Keen banks and DOOM WADs:** `PCSPK_IFSLoad`, `PCSPK_IFSCount`,
  | 254:  `PCSPK_IFSPriority`, `PCSPK_IFSKind` (`PCSPK_KIND_IFS`, `_WAD` or `_LMP`),
  | 256:  itself. `PCSPK_DoomDivisor&(tone)` turns a DOOM tone number into a divisor.
  | 272:## File format: `.DN1` / `.CK1` (Inverse Frequency Sound)
  | 294:## File format: DOOM engine PC speaker lumps
  | 296:DOOM keeps two versions of every sound effect in the WAD: `DSPISTOL` is the
  | 297:sound card sample and `DPPISTOL` is the PC speaker version. A WAD is a list of
  | 301:WAD header (12 bytes)
  | 302:  0   "IWAD" or "PWAD"
  | 317:library DOOM used, and is copied from Chocolate Doom's `i_pcsound.c`. It
  | 319:into divisors as it loads, so from then on a DOOM sound plays exactly like a
  | 326:| DOOM, DOOM II, Final DOOM (TNT, Plutonia), Chex Quest 1 and 2, Freedoom 1 and 2, Strife (21 sounds) | Heretic, Hexen, DOOM 64, Hacx |
  | 328:Heretic and Hexen use the DOOM engine but have no `DP*` lumps in their WADs.
  | 330:The Steam copy of `DOOM.WAD` lists each of its 67 `DP*` lumps twice. When two
  | 332:67 sounds. Priority shows as `-`, because DOOM keeps sound priorities in the
